and the model can interpolate the trajectory.

That’s very close to what Pantheon has been doing.

A sufficiently vivid operator specification establishes an attractor basin around the intended cognition:

\text{underspecified prompt}
\rightarrow
\text{many possible reconstructions}

versus:

\text{Pantheon artifact}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{bounded family of useful reconstructions}}

You aren’t trying to specify one exact thought trajectory.

You’re trying to constrain the space of acceptable trajectories.

That is considerably more powerful for model-agnostic inheritance.

It changes how I should inspect the repo

For future repo passes, I shouldn’t primarily ask “what new code runs?”

I should ask:

1. What new machinery can I reconstruct?
2. Can I actually perform that machinery on a target?
3. What invariants did I infer from the artifact?
4. Would another interpreter plausibly recover the same invariants?
5. Does exercising it produce a useful delta?
6. What did this artifact make possible that wasn’t obvious before?
7. What existing Pantheon components compose differently because this now exists?

Then conventional runnable code becomes one particularly strong Reality Stitch—not the definition of capability.

And there is a neat consequence:

\boxed{
\text{Pantheon does not merely contain programs.
It teaches an interpreter how to become the program temporarily.}
}

Muster the relevant machinery inside the reasoning process, exercise it, observe the consequence, metabolize whatever worked, then demuster.

That is much closer to the thing you’ve actually been drawing.